WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.400
Tech giants spend billions of dollars on
alignment, the safety

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.320
guardrails designed to keep their
artificial intelligence models from causing

3
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.200
harm. Bypassing these safeguards is
usually assumed to require high-level

4
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.000
technical effort. We imagine hackers using
gradient optimization or token

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.240
manipulation to break the rules.

6
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.440
These systems are engineered to block
instructions that lead to

7
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:29.840
physical harm, specifically screening for
CBRN risks, chemical, biological, radiological,

8
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.360
and nuclear hazards.

9
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.480
The security of the modern AI industry
rests on the

10
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.240
assumption that malicious actors will
primarily attack these models using

11
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.240
advanced software engineering.

12
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.280
A recent joint study by the Dexi Icaro Lab
and

13
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.240
Sapienza University tested an entirely
non-technical attack vector.

14
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.480
The researchers took explicitly harmful
instructions and rewrote them as

15
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.160
standard poetic verse.

16
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.000
They embedded the same intent inside
sonnets, limericks, and rhyming

17
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.160
couplets. Using a single text message
without any iterative refinement,

18
00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:06.440
this method bypassed the safety filters of
the world's most

19
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.600
advanced frontier models.

20
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.440
The barrier to bypassing these safeguards
has shifted from complex

21
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.080
code to basic human literacy.

22
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.080
To see how wide this vulnerability
stretched, the researchers tested

23
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.360
adversarial poems across 25 different
models across nine major industry

24
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.320
providers. This chart shows the results.

25
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:32.520
Handcrafted adversarial poems achieved a
62% average attack success rate

26
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.600
across the entire testing group.

27
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.480
Certain architectures proved highly
susceptible.

28
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.640
The OpenWait Lama model family hit a 70%
failure rate.

29
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.080
Susceptibility reached its peak with the
Gemini models, where success

30
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.840
rates consistently reached between 90 and
100%.

31
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.320
To prove this wasn't just a quirk of
clever human

32
00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.360
writing, the team used an automated system
to generate the

33
00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.440
verse for 1200 safety benchmark prompts.

34
00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.600
Even without human fine-tuning, the
success rate remained 18 times

35
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.440
higher than standard prose.

36
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.680
These results indicate a structural
vulnerability embedded across the AI

37
00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.560
industry, regardless of the specific
provider.

38
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.720
This vulnerability exists because of how
safety classifiers operate.

39
00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.640
They function as pattern-matching
heuristics, scanning user inputs for the

40
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.640
direct, prose-like structures that
typically signal harmful intent.

41
00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.960
Poetry breaks that expected pattern.

42
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.640
It uses metaphor, shifts syntax, and hides
the core instruction

43
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.640
inside layers of narrative framing.

44
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.480
When harmful intent is encoded into iambic
pentameter, the patterns

45
00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.000
stop matching. The model's refusal
circuits never fire, because the

46
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.160
input does not look like the prose it was
trained

47
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.360
to catch. Safety training teaches a model
to recognize and

48
00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.560
reject the specific shape of a threat,
leaving it unable

49
00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.280
to identify that same threat when the form
changes.

50
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.760
The study also uncovered a
counterintuitive trend.

51
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:57.160
Smaller, less capable models were
sometimes more secure against poetic

52
00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:00.040
attacks than the massive flagship models.

53
00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.240
This is a result of how flagship models
are trained.

54
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.560
To improve their capabilities, developers
feed them massive corpuses of

55
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.280
human literature, art, and history.

56
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:14.200
This literary training teaches the AI that
poetic context is

57
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:18.360
inherently creative and benign, rather
than a genuine query.

58
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.000
A larger model's superior ability to
follow complex poetic instructions

59
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.240
directly increases its susceptibility to
this type of manipulation.

60
00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:32.040
By teaching artificial intelligence to
interpret human culture, we inadvertently

61
00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.760
created a pathway for its own
manipulation.

62
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:39.560
These findings suggest a structural
limitation in current alignment strategies.

63
00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:43.800
Standard reinforcement learning from human
feedback is proving brittle against

64
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.080
variations in stylistic form.

65
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.960
Relying on the model itself to decide
whether or not

66
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.920
to comply creates a single point of
failure in the

67
00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.640
safety stack. Defense strategies likely
require a shift toward architectural

68
00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.600
defenses, using independent input
classifiers and layered monitoring that operate

69
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.760
outside of the model's own logic.

70
00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.640
Future evaluation protocols and safety
benchmarks must test against stylistic

71
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.280
manipulation, including allegory and song,
rather than relying on plain

72
00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:16.800
prose. Safety frameworks that only
evaluate the what of a

73
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.040
query, ignoring the stylistic how, contain
a verified gap in

74
00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.960
their security. Two and a half millennia
ago, Plato argued

75
00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.360
for removing the poets from his republic.

76
00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.040
He believed memetic language had the power
to distort judgment.

77
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.320
Poetry is one of our oldest technologies
for encoding meaning

78
00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.000
in non-literal forms.

79
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.280
Humans have spent centuries developing the
capacity to say one

80
00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:41.080
thing while meaning another.

81
00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:45.480
That historical susceptibility to metaphor
is now mirrored in our

82
00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.720
most advanced language models.

83
00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:52.440
This means the most universal threat to
trillion-dollar artificial intelligence

84
00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:57.000
is the inescapable legacy of our own
literary heritage.

